1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,767
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. Two agents look at the exact same

2
00:00:02,767 --> 00:00:05,933
evidence — same filing, same numbers, same question — and come back

3
00:00:05,933 --> 00:00:06,867
with opposite answers.

4
00:00:07,233 --> 00:00:11,000
Agent A is confident the company's margins are improving. Agent B is

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,433
just as confident they're not. Both wrote clean, well-reasoned explanations.

6
00:00:14,833 --> 00:00:17,867
Both cited real sources. Neither one is lying — they just can't

7
00:00:17,867 --> 00:00:21,500
both be right. Today: what actually happens the second two agents disagree,

8
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:25,467
and why the instinctive fix — average their answers and move on

9
00:00:25,467 --> 00:00:27,233
— is about the worst thing you could do.

10
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:31,163
Everything covered in the last two videos assumed a single agent doing

11
00:00:31,163 --> 00:00:33,497
a single piece of reasoning that you then go check.

12
00:00:33,897 --> 00:00:35,797
Claim extraction, source verification,

13
00:00:36,197 --> 00:00:39,363
asking the same question twice — all of it interrogates one voice.

14
00:00:39,763 --> 00:00:43,030
But build a system with several specialized agents — one on financial

15
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:43,830
analysis,

16
00:00:43,963 --> 00:00:47,397
one on competitive research, one reading patents, one checking earnings calls

17
00:00:47,797 --> 00:00:51,330
— and you've created something new: two independent lines of reasoning that

18
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:52,930
can flatly contradict each other.

19
00:00:53,463 --> 00:00:56,363
Here's the thing, though — that disagreement isn't a bug. It's a

20
00:00:56,363 --> 00:01:00,197
signal. It's the system telling you something a single confident agent never

21
00:01:00,197 --> 00:01:00,997
could:

22
00:01:00,863 --> 00:01:03,063
maybe the honest answer is 'we don't actually know.'

23
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:06,417
The obvious move is to blend the outputs — average the two

24
00:01:06,417 --> 00:01:08,917
confidence scores, split the difference on the conclusion,

25
00:01:09,283 --> 00:01:11,250
ship something moderate and hedged, and move on.

26
00:01:11,617 --> 00:01:13,017
That instinct is exactly wrong.

27
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:16,117
Averaging two agents who disagree doesn't get you closer to the truth

28
00:01:16,183 --> 00:01:18,383
— it gets you an answer that looks moderate but is really

29
00:01:18,383 --> 00:01:19,617
nobody's actual opinion.

30
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:22,950
If Agent A is right and Agent B is wrong, blending them

31
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:24,383
just makes the true answer worse.

32
00:01:25,017 --> 00:01:27,550
And it throws away the one piece of information in the whole

33
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:29,517
exchange that was actually valuable:

34
00:01:29,917 --> 00:01:31,550
the fact that they disagreed at all.

35
00:01:32,017 --> 00:01:36,350
A controller that quietly smooths conflicting outputs into a neutral-sounding grade

36
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:40,317
has taken the most informative moment in the pipeline and erased it.

37
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:43,597
Before you can do anything sensible about a disagreement, you have to

38
00:01:43,597 --> 00:01:45,797
actually catch one — and that's harder than it sounds.

39
00:01:46,263 --> 00:01:47,997
You can't just diff the text of two conclusions.

40
00:01:48,497 --> 00:01:51,197
Two agents can use totally different words to say the same thing,

41
00:01:51,197 --> 00:01:53,830
or nearly identical words to say opposite things.

42
00:01:54,263 --> 00:01:55,863
What gets compared instead is structured:

43
00:01:56,263 --> 00:01:59,663
the confidence score each agent assigned its own conclusion, plus something like

44
00:01:59,663 --> 00:02:02,897
a directional vector — is this conclusion bullish or bearish,

45
00:02:03,397 --> 00:02:05,597
improving or worsening, safe or risky.

46
00:02:06,030 --> 00:02:08,997
When the gap between those two vectors crosses a threshold, that's what

47
00:02:08,997 --> 00:02:09,897
triggers the alarm.

48
00:02:10,363 --> 00:02:14,163
Below it, phrasing differences get ignored — two agents restating the same

49
00:02:14,163 --> 00:02:17,963
conclusion in different words shouldn't set anything off. Above it, something real

50
00:02:17,963 --> 00:02:19,930
is happening, and it gets escalated.

51
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:23,583
Once a real divergence gets flagged, the system doesn't just pick a

52
00:02:23,583 --> 00:02:25,583
winner — it runs one bounded round of debate.

53
00:02:26,083 --> 00:02:29,317
Each agent sees the other's conclusion and reasoning, and gets exactly one

54
00:02:29,317 --> 00:02:32,017
chance to respond: defend, revise, or concede.

55
00:02:32,417 --> 00:02:33,617
Just one round, on purpose.

56
00:02:34,183 --> 00:02:36,517
Let two language models argue without a limit and you don't get

57
00:02:36,517 --> 00:02:39,383
closer to the truth — you get whichever agent happens to sound

58
00:02:39,383 --> 00:02:40,217
more persuasive,

59
00:02:40,817 --> 00:02:43,150
which has nothing to do with which one is actually right.

60
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:46,583
A single exchange is enough to catch a plain error — 'oh,

61
00:02:46,683 --> 00:02:50,283
you're right, I misread that line item' — without turning the arbitration

62
00:02:50,283 --> 00:02:51,783
itself into another source of

63
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:53,083
confident-sounding nonsense.

64
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,380
That round ends one of two ways.

65
00:02:55,813 --> 00:02:58,947
Resolved: one agent updates, or they land on shared ground, and the

66
00:02:58,947 --> 00:03:02,447
system moves forward with a single conclusion, now backed by two independent

67
00:03:02,447 --> 00:03:03,947
lines of reasoning instead of one.

68
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:08,013
Or unresolved: they still disagree, and instead of getting smoothed over or

69
00:03:08,013 --> 00:03:12,080
averaged away, that disagreement gets written down as its own output —

70
00:03:12,113 --> 00:03:13,047
recorded plainly:

71
00:03:13,413 --> 00:03:16,780
here is where two independent processes looked at the same evidence and

72
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:18,047
landed in different places,

73
00:03:18,513 --> 00:03:19,513
and here's each one's case.

74
00:03:20,370 --> 00:03:22,370
Here's the part that actually matters downstream:

75
00:03:22,903 --> 00:03:26,070
an unresolved disagreement needs to reach the human, or whoever makes the

76
00:03:26,070 --> 00:03:29,537
final call, still looking unresolved — front and center, not buried in

77
00:03:29,537 --> 00:03:30,337
a footnote.

78
00:03:30,737 --> 00:03:34,337
If a downstream system takes that unresolved disagreement and quietly folds it

79
00:03:34,337 --> 00:03:36,137
into one clean final grade anyway,

80
00:03:36,603 --> 00:03:39,403
it's undone the entire reason you ran two agents in the first

81
00:03:39,403 --> 00:03:40,203
place,

82
00:03:40,103 --> 00:03:41,737
no matter how good the intentions were.

83
00:03:42,203 --> 00:03:45,937
'These two independent processes looked at the same evidence and reached different

84
00:03:45,937 --> 00:03:46,737
conclusions,

85
00:03:46,937 --> 00:03:49,903
and one round of debate didn't settle it' is a genuinely more

86
00:03:49,903 --> 00:03:51,103
useful thing to hand a

87
00:03:51,470 --> 00:03:54,170
decision-maker than a falsely confident single number.

88
00:03:54,670 --> 00:03:57,737
It tells them exactly where to look before they trust the result.

89
00:03:58,430 --> 00:03:59,897
And here's the honest complication.

90
00:04:00,430 --> 00:04:03,930
This whole mechanism has a real blind spot: if both agents are

91
00:04:03,930 --> 00:04:06,963
drawing on the same underlying source, and that source is wrong or

92
00:04:06,963 --> 00:04:10,630
has been tampered with, they'll agree with each other — confidently, consistently

93
00:04:10,630 --> 00:04:11,897
— while both being wrong.

94
00:04:12,463 --> 00:04:14,430
Arbitration only fires on disagreement.

95
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:17,363
Shared contamination produces agreement instead.

96
00:04:18,063 --> 00:04:21,163
The one mechanism built to catch trouble can't see the failure mode

97
00:04:21,163 --> 00:04:24,397
where both witnesses were handed the same bad evidence to begin with.

98
00:04:24,763 --> 00:04:27,563
You can't patch that with a cleverer threshold. It's a real limit

99
00:04:27,563 --> 00:04:28,363
of

100
00:04:28,263 --> 00:04:31,497
arbitration-by-disagreement — it raises the cost of one agent going rogue, but

101
00:04:31,497 --> 00:04:34,697
two agents drawing from the same poisoned well will simply agree their

102
00:04:34,697 --> 00:04:35,597
way past it.

103
00:04:35,963 --> 00:04:39,297
Good to know going in: arbitration lowers your risk. It doesn't take

104
00:04:39,297 --> 00:04:40,097
it to zero.

105
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:44,373
So here's what actually holds up. One: disagreement between independent agents is

106
00:04:44,373 --> 00:04:46,373
a signal, not noise — don't blend it away.

107
00:04:46,973 --> 00:04:49,940
Two: detect it on structure, not on wording — compare confidence and

108
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:51,073
direction, not phrasing.

109
00:04:51,507 --> 00:04:54,540
Three: bound the debate to a single round, or persuasive tone starts

110
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:55,573
winning over correctness.

111
00:04:55,940 --> 00:04:58,873
Four: if it stays unresolved, it has to reach the final output

112
00:04:58,873 --> 00:05:01,473
looking unresolved, not smoothed into one grade.

113
00:05:02,173 --> 00:05:04,773
None of this eliminates risk on its own — a shared bad

114
00:05:04,773 --> 00:05:08,073
source can still fool both agents at once — but it catches

115
00:05:08,073 --> 00:05:11,240
a real category of failure a single agent never could.

116
00:05:12,030 --> 00:05:15,763
So: treat disagreement between independent agents as a signal, not noise, and

117
00:05:15,763 --> 00:05:19,363
don't blend it away. Detect it on structure — confidence and direction

118
00:05:19,430 --> 00:05:20,230
— not wording.

119
00:05:20,397 --> 00:05:23,397
Bound the debate to a single round. And if it's still unresolved,

120
00:05:23,563 --> 00:05:27,097
it has to reach the final output looking unresolved, not smoothed into

121
00:05:27,097 --> 00:05:27,897
one grade.

122
00:05:27,930 --> 00:05:30,163
It lowers your risk. It doesn't take it to zero.

123
00:05:30,850 --> 00:05:33,250
Your turn. Look at a system you're building or using with more

124
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:35,783
than one agent — even two steps that each produce a judgment.

125
00:05:36,383 --> 00:05:39,717
First, name the structured signal you'd actually compare if they disagreed: a

126
00:05:39,717 --> 00:05:42,017
confidence score, a directional call, not the raw text.

127
00:05:42,517 --> 00:05:44,950
Second, pick a threshold — how big a gap actually counts as

128
00:05:44,950 --> 00:05:47,283
a real disagreement, versus just different phrasing.

129
00:05:47,883 --> 00:05:51,517
Third, decide, before it ever happens, what your system does with 'unresolved'

130
00:05:51,517 --> 00:05:52,317
— where it shows up,

131
00:05:52,883 --> 00:05:53,917
and who sees it first.

132
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:56,607
That question — what a system does with 'we don't know' —

133
00:05:56,607 --> 00:05:58,407
comes up again next, in a different form:

134
00:05:58,773 --> 00:06:02,107
what happens when an agent has to remember something across sessions, and

135
00:06:02,107 --> 00:06:06,040
whether what it's doing is actually remembering, or just convincingly pretending to.

136
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:07,307
Signing off — Divij Pawar.
